   =========================================================
   CSS NOTES: THE CASCADE (DEEP EXPLANATION)

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   What is the CSS Cascade?
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   The cascade is the decision-making system CSS uses
   when more than one style applies to the same element.

   CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets.
   "Cascading" means styles flow together and conflicts
   are resolved using a strict set of rules.

   The cascade answers one question:
   Which CSS rule should the browser actually apply?

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   The Cascade Works Using FOUR FACTORS
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   1. Importance (!important)
   2. Origin (where the style comes from)
   3. Specificity (how precise the selector is)
   4. Source Order (which rule appears last)

   The browser checks these in EXACTLY this order.

   =========================================================
   1. IMPORTANCE (!important) — FIRST CHECK
   =========================================================
   Importance is the strongest signal in CSS.

   When multiple rules apply, the browser first asks:
   "Is any rule marked with !important?"

   If yes:
   - All normal rules are ignored
   - Only rules with !important are considered

   !important can override:
   - Element selectors
   - Class selectors
   - ID selectors
   - Inline styles

   If TWO rules both have !important:
   - The browser moves to specificity to decide

   Why !important is dangerous:
   - Breaks the natural cascade
   - Makes CSS hard to override
   - Causes debugging problems

   =========================================================
   2. ORIGIN — WHERE THE STYLE COMES FROM
   =========================================================
   If importance is equal, the browser checks origin.

   There are three main origins:

   1. Browser default styles
      (Example: links are blue, headings are bold)

   2. Author styles (your CSS)
      - External CSS files
      - Internal <style> blocks
      - Inline styles

   3. User styles (rare, accessibility related)

   Priority order (simplified):
   - Browser styles → lowest
   - Author styles → higher
   - Inline styles → highest (among author styles)

   This means:
   - Your CSS overrides browser defaults
   - Inline styles override your CSS

   Origin is checked BEFORE specificity.

   =========================================================
   3. SPECIFICITY — HOW PRECISE THE SELECTOR IS
   =========================================================
   If importance and origin are the same, the browser
   checks specificity.

   Specificity is NOT about:
   - Selector length
   - Visual nesting
   - File size

   Specificity IS about selector type.

   Priority order:
   - Inline styles
   - ID selectors
   - Class / attribute / pseudo-class selectors
   - Element / pseudo-element selectors

   Key ideas:
   - A more precise rule beats a general rule
   - ID beats class
   - Class beats element

   Specificity decides most real-world CSS conflicts.

   =========================================================
   4. SOURCE ORDER — LAST RULE WINS (FINAL STEP)
   =========================================================
   If all of the following are equal:
   - Importance
   - Origin
   - Specificity

   Then the browser checks source order.

   Rule:
   - The rule written LAST wins

   This is why:
   - Later CSS files override earlier ones
   - Lower rules override upper rules in the same file

   Source order is the FINAL tie-breaker, not the main rule.

   =========================================================
   HOW ALL FOUR WORK TOGETHER
   =========================================================
   The browser follows this exact thinking process:

   1. Is !important used?
   2. Where does the style come from?
   3. Which selector is more specific?
   4. Which rule is written last?

   As soon as a winner is found, all other rules
   are ignored.

   =========================================================
   COMMON BEGINNER MISUNDERSTANDING
   =========================================================
   Many people think CSS is random or confusing.

   In reality:
   - CSS is strict
   - CSS is predictable
   - CSS always follows the cascade rules

   Understanding the cascade helps you:
   - Avoid !important misuse
   - Debug faster
   - Write cleaner CSS

   =========================================================
   MENTAL MODEL
   =========================================================
   Think of the cascade like a courtroom:

   - !important → emergency law
   - Origin → jurisdiction
   - Specificity → authority rank
   - Order → final tie-break decision

   =========================================================
   ONE-LINE SUMMARY
   =========================================================
   The cascade is the complete rule system CSS uses
   to decide which style wins when multiple rules
   target the same element.

